{
  "format": "horde.gui3-layout/1",
  "note": "GUI 3's page layout (B584, ADR-316). Design input, hand-edited: which parameter goes on which page and in which group. It names parameters by key and nothing else; labels, ranges, laws, defaults and options come from SPEC-ENGINE section 5 through tools/gen_gui3_controls.py, which refuses a key the spec does not have, a host parameter no page places, and a reserved row placed anywhere. A group's `block` places every host row of that block in the spec table's order (5.8: a table's row order is its display order), so a row added to the spec appears on its draft page without an edit here. A `designed` page lists its keys one by one, so a new row there is unplaced, and red, until the page's designer places it. `live` is PA-8's live-when table, transcribed for the designed pages only; a control whose condition fails is drawn idle (dashed), never hidden. `widget` overrides the widget rule (ADR-319 item 5, gui3/README.md) for a row, and must say why in `reason`; a knob or a slider for a continuous row is the rule, not an override. `panel` names a shell-built panel a page carries beside its generated controls. `bank` chooses, per modulator kind, the critical controls the bottom modulator bank's cards carry (ADR-322 item 3). `sources` names every parameter source beside SPEC-ENGINE 5 that has a machine-readable manifest today (ADR-330): each with its vendored file (relative to docs/design/gui3), its format, and where its values go (`rack` for the FX modules, RC-5 and IF-7; `midi` for Arp, the device's MIDI module). Appendix D.2's morph settings are read from the spec itself and go to the morph resolver (IF-12). `owed` lists the sources with no manifest yet; nothing is invented for them. A group's `screen` names the module whose own screen will sit in the card's inset (a labelled placeholder until then). B584 slice 4b (ADR-329, the design master's density revision B589): `nav` is the two-level page bar (groups over their pages); a `dense` page draws each group as a Section header inside an `.hd-cell` (the one box level), with `sub` labels for clusters, a control's `size` (primary, compact, utility; compact is the dense default) and `advanced` (inside a collapsed Advanced sub-group, still mounted); a group's `num` and `rule` (1 solid, 2 dash, 3 dot) tell repeated sections apart; a control's `role` puts it in the section header (`power`: the on toggle, `modes`: the mode pills) or a channel strip (`fader`, `on`, `mute`, `solo`). A group with a `block` may give `meta` per block suffix and `rest` (`advanced`) for the block's other rows. `viz` names the page's or card's SAMPLE picture. The dashboard's cards name their `source` page: a card's controls are the same keys as that page's (one model, two views). A `viz` is `{kind, keys}`: the picture's kind and the parameter keys it reads, so the shell names no key of its own; the picture is a SAMPLE drawing of those settings, never the engine's output. A group's `follow` (ADR-333) shows the envelope a source's level follows (RC-6.5): its `selector` (the source's `env` row) picks one of `blocks` by option, and the group carries that block's `suffixes` for every block, so each key is a real, shared row (the Mod page's and Main's own), the shell shows the selected block's and the picture reads its `viz` rows. A `meta` entry `elsewhere` leaves a block row to another group of the same page.",
  "bank": {
    "note": "Which modulators the bank lists is never kept here: it is read from the adapter's routes (RC-4) against the current page's keys, plus the modulators activated with + and not yet mapped. Only the choice of each kind's critical controls is design input.",
    "kinds": [
      {
        "kind": "env",
        "blocks": "env[1-4]",
        "suffixes": [
          "attack",
          "decay",
          "sustain",
          "release"
        ],
        "reason": "An envelope's shape is its four stage controls: attack, decay, sustain and release say what it does to a route at a glance, and every one of them moves the sound. Hold, the three curves, velocity and one-shot refine that shape, and stay on the envelope's own page (Main for ENV 1, Mod for ENV 2 to ENV 4)."
      }
    ]
  },
